1
00:00:00,502 --> 00:00:04,911
Follow a new civil filing from a saved draft to its submission receipt.

2
00:00:07,002 --> 00:00:10,314
Sign in as an attorney to reach your filing workspace.

3
00:00:10,985 --> 00:00:15,446
Start a new filing, or return to work already saved to your account.

4
00:00:17,535 --> 00:00:22,291
Choose the destination court, then tell the portal whether this begins a new case.

5
00:00:22,962 --> 00:00:27,885
For this demonstration, select the Third Circuit Court and a general civil case.

6
00:00:28,554 --> 00:00:32,255
The case type brings its document requirements into view.

7
00:00:34,335 --> 00:00:36,978
Enter the case title and a short description.

8
00:00:37,648 --> 00:00:43,396
Continuing saves a draft to the attorney's account before documents are uploaded, giving this filing a

9
00:00:43,396 --> 00:00:44,893
stable identity.

10
00:00:47,002 --> 00:00:49,992
Add the complaint and summons as separate documents.

11
00:00:50,676 --> 00:00:54,066
Saved uploads stay listed with their types and sizes.

12
00:00:54,735 --> 00:00:58,822
Review the list, and remove a mistaken upload before submitting.

13
00:01:00,902 --> 00:01:05,812
The final review brings the saved case information and attached documents together.

14
00:01:06,483 --> 00:01:12,026
Check the filing itself, then confirm that the required-document validation has passed.

15
00:01:14,102 --> 00:01:17,968
This demo uses a synthetic fee of one hundred fifty dollars.

16
00:01:18,652 --> 00:01:23,601
Recording the simulated payment collects no card details and charges no money.

17
00:01:24,271 --> 00:01:28,628
The review updates automatically, and the filing is ready to submit.

18
00:01:30,702 --> 00:01:37,868
Submit the filing. The confirmation gives the filing number, case title, document count, and its current

19
00:01:37,868 --> 00:01:39,339
submitted status.

20
00:01:41,435 --> 00:01:44,657
Open the retained receipt to see what was received and when.

21
00:01:45,328 --> 00:01:48,370
Submission and court acceptance are separate events.

22
00:01:49,039 --> 00:01:52,315
The court's later decision appears in this same history.

23
00:01:54,402 --> 00:01:58,462
Back in My Filings, the new submission is listed with its status.

24
00:01:59,132 --> 00:02:01,776
The attorney has a clear record to follow.

25
00:02:03,868 --> 00:02:07,374
The attorney has a submitted filing and a retained receipt.

26
00:02:08,045 --> 00:02:14,207
This is a demonstration with fictional records, simulated payment, and a stub court-system connection.
